sections.licensing_pathway:
**Step 1 — Form the entity and plan the building.** Secondary guides report a $99 Articles of Organization fee and a $39 name reservation for 180 days. These are not from the Secretary of State. Confirm the fees there. Secure a building with local zoning clearance. The application requires a statement of the facility's status under applicable zoning ordinances or rules, or a statement that no zoning authority exists [S5]. Local zoning and siting rules were not researched.

**Step 2 — Assemble the application.** Per OAC 3701-16-03, the initial application must include [S5]:
- a floor plan of the area to be licensed, including beds;
- disclosure of owners with 5% or more interest, any management firm, and a statutory agent;
- a certificate of occupancy (use group R-4, I-1 or I-2);
- an inspection report from the state fire marshal or an approved fire department;
- a heating inspection report;
- a CPA-signed financial solvency statement showing capital or reserve to cover not less than three months of operation.

ODH publishes the current initial application packet (dated 04/30/2026) and other forms [S2][S10]. Questions go to the Licensure Unit at liccert@odh.ohio.gov or (614) 466-7713 [S2].

**Step 3 — File and pay.** File the application at least 60 days before the proposed opening [S5]. The non-refundable fee is $320 for each 50 beds or part thereof of licensed capacity [S6][S10]. It is paid with the completed application, by check or money order payable to 'treasurer, state of Ohio' [S5]. Every size modeled here (17–50 beds) falls in one fee block. The 2026 packet table was not read directly. The amount comes from ORC 3721.02, consistent with the 2018 and 2026-dated packets.

**Step 4 — Inspections.** Before a license issues, ODH and the state fire marshal (or an approved fire department) must inspect, except for ownership transfers [S6]. You may request an expedited inspection. ODH must begin it within 10 business days of a complete request. An extra fee may apply, and no amount was found [S6]. ODH's review timeline after the inspection was not found. Do not assume a short approval period. Plan on the 60-day minimum plus the inspection scheduling.

**Step 5 — Renewal and ongoing inspection.** Renewal needs a completed renewal application and the same non-refundable fee [S5]. A 2024-cycle ODH notice carried a late fee of $100 per week or part thereof [S35]. That figure is not confirmed for the current cycle. Under ORC 3721.02, ODH inspects at least once every 15 months. The director may extend this to once every 30 months for an RCF that meets listed clean-record conditions. Fire inspections are required at least every 15 months [S6].

**Step 6 — Change of operator.** An application is due at least 60 days before the proposed assignment or transfer of the license, with a non-refundable fee [S5].

**Step 7 — Medicaid waiver (optional).** Beyond the ODH license, a facility needs ODA certification as an Assisted Living Waiver provider [S12]. The steps, fees and timelines for ODA certification were not found. Ask ODA before you commit capital. ODM provider enrollment is electronic only [S23]. The 2026 organizational application fee is $750 [S21][S22]. It is waived if you already paid the fee to Medicare or another state's Medicaid program within the past two years, with proof [S21]. ODM has paused new enrollment for home health, hospice and related provider types from May 14 to November 14, 2026. The notice does not say whether RCFs are covered [S24]. Confirm before you apply.
